(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["llmserve",{"l:llmserve/how-does-pagedattention-store-a-growing-kv-cache":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A request's KV cache grows by one token per decoding step and nobody knows its final length. PagedAttention stores it in fixed-size \u003cb>blocks\u003c/b> taken from a shared pool on demand, and each sequence keeps a \u003cb>block table\u003c/b> that maps its logical block i to a physical block anywhere in memory. Blocks carry \u003cb>reference counts\u003c/b>, so forked sequences share them, and a write to a shared block \u003cb>copies it first\u003c/b>. Waste falls to at most one partly filled block per sequence: the same trade operating systems made with pages.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A hotel hosts conference groups whose size it learns only as people arrive. The old policy reserves a whole corridor for every group, sized for the largest group the hotel has ever seen; most rooms stay empty, and the leftover corridors come in awkward sizes nobody else can use. The new policy hands out rooms one at a time, anywhere in the building, and gives each group a room list. A group never holds more than one half-empty room, and two groups that share a meeting can point at the same room until one of them needs to change it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The KV cache is the guest list. For every token in a sequence, each layer stores a key vector and a value vector so that later tokens can attend to it without recomputing. Earlier serving systems stored each request's cache contiguously and pre-reserved room for its maximum length. The PagedAttention paper profiled them: only \u003cb>20.4% to 38.2%\u003c/b> of their KV memory held actual token states, against \u003cb>96.3%\u003c/b> for vLLM (Kwon et al. 2023, Figure 2).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The pieces, next to the operating system\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operating-system virtual memory\u003c/th>\u003cth>PagedAttention\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Page (for example 4 KiB)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>KV block of B tokens (vLLM's default B is 16)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Process\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sequence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Page table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Block table: a list of physical block ids\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Frame allocated on first touch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Block allocated when the previous one is full\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>fork()\u003c/code> shares pages copy-on-write\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parallel samples and beam candidates share blocks copy-on-write\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Swap pages to disk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Swap blocks to CPU memory, or drop them and recompute\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Address translation\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The token at position \u003ccode>pos\u003c/code> lives in physical block \u003ccode>table[pos // B]\u003c/code> at offset \u003ccode>pos % B\u003c/code>. The attention kernel does exactly this lookup when it gathers keys and values, which is the part the name \"PagedAttention\" refers to.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Append, fork and free\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Append.\u003c/b> Compute \u003ccode>slot = n % B\u003c/code>. If \u003ccode>slot == 0\u003c/code>, the sequence has no block yet or its last block is full, so pop a block from the free list. Otherwise the token goes into the last block, unless that block's reference count is above one: then \u003cb>copy on write\u003c/b> first (allocate a new block, copy the \u003ccode>slot\u003c/code> filled entries, drop one reference to the old block, point the table at the copy).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fork.\u003c/b> Copy the table, which is a short list of integers, and add one to the reference count of every block in it. No key or value is copied.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Free.\u003c/b> Drop one reference per block; a block whose count reaches zero goes back to the free list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Blocks fill from left to right and only the last one is ever written, so every earlier block is immutable once full. That is why copy-on-write never copies more than one block per fork, as the paper notes for beam search.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>When the pool runs dry\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Sequences grow at every step, so a batch that fitted can stop fitting. The paper's answer (§4.5) is preemption with an all-or-nothing rule: evict every block of a sequence or none, then either swap the blocks to CPU memory or simply free them and recompute the cache later from the prompt plus the tokens generated so far. vLLM's current engine recomputes. The continuous batching card simulates this.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Sharing across requests\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The same reference counts let one cached prompt prefix appear in many block tables, which is how prefix caching reuses work across requests (next card). vLLM's V1 design keeps block tables append-only and shares only full blocks through its prefix cache.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Memory per token.\u003c/b> Each layer stores one key and one value vector per KV head, so bytes per token = 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes per value. For 32 layers, 8 KV heads of dimension 128 and 16-bit values that is 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes, 128 KiB per token, and a 16-token block is 2 MiB. The AI guide's KV-cache memory lesson works through real model configs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Waste.\u003c/b> Reserving \u003ccode>max_len\u003c/code> contiguously wastes \u003ccode>max_len - n\u003c/code> slots per sequence. Paging wastes only the unfilled tail of the last block: at most B − 1 slots, about B/2 on average when lengths are random. On a synthetic workload of 1,000 requests (log-normal lengths, mean 541 tokens, capped at 2,048), contiguous reservation put tokens in 26.4% of reserved slots; paging with B = 16 put tokens in 98.6%, wasting 7.5 slots per sequence on average, close to B/2 = 8.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Operations.\u003c/b> Append is O(1), plus one O(B) block copy the first time a forked sequence writes. Fork and free are O(number of blocks), which is n/B, instead of copying O(n) vectors. Translation is O(1) but adds an indirection to the attention kernel: the paper measured 20 to 26% higher attention-kernel latency than FasterTransformer's contiguous kernel (§7.1), repaid many times over by larger batches.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reach for paging when many variable-length buffers grow unpredictably inside one fixed pool, or when buffers must share long common parts cheaply.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth>Waste per sequence\u003c/th>\u003cth>Growth\u003c/th>\u003cth>Fork or share\u003c/th>\u003cth>Read\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reserve the maximum length contiguously\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_len - n\u003c/code> slots, plus external fragmentation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>free until the cap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>copy everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>direct\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grow a contiguous buffer by doubling, like a Python list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>up to n slots of slack, plus copies on every growth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>amortised O(1) with copying\u003c/td>\u003ctd>copy everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>direct\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Paged blocks with a block table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>under B slots\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1), no copying\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n/B) table copy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one table lookup\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not bother when every buffer has the same known size (fixed-length classification inputs, say): a contiguous arena wastes nothing and avoids the indirection. Block size is itself a trade-off. Too small and the kernel cannot read enough in parallel; too large and internal fragmentation rises and less is shared. The paper found 16 to 128 best on one trace, while on a trace of short sequences larger blocks degraded performance, so vLLM chose 16 (§7.2).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Block size 4. The free list hands out blocks 7, 1, 3, 0, ... in that order, which reproduces Figures 6 and 8 of the paper: a 7-token prompt, then two samples that share it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A writes a 7-token prompt: slots 0 and 4 start new blocks, so A takes block 7, then block 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A table  [7, 1]   n = 7\nblock 7  t0 t1 t2 t3     ref 1\nblock 1  t4 t5 t6 __     ref 1\nfree     next out: 3, 0, 2, ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Fork B from A for a second sample: copy the table, add a reference to each block. Nothing else moves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A table  [7, 1]   B table  [7, 1]\nblock 7  t0 t1 t2 t3     ref \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nblock 1  t4 t5 t6 __     ref \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A appends a7 at slot 7 % 4 = 3 of block 1, but block 1 is shared: copy on write into block 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A table  [7, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]   B table  [7, 1]\nblock 3  t4 t5 t6 \u003cmark>a7\u003c/mark>   ref 1\nblock 1  t4 t5 t6 __     ref \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">B appends b7: block 1 now has one owner, so B writes in place with no copy\u003c/p>\u003cpre>B table  [7, 1]\nblock 1  t4 t5 t6 \u003cmark>b7\u003c/mark>   ref 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">B appends b8 at slot 8 % 4 = 0: its last block is full, so it takes a fresh block (0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>B table  [7, 1, \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>]\nblock 0  \u003cmark>b8\u003c/mark> __ __ __   ref 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read A's position 5: block A.table[5 // 4] = A.table[1] = 3, offset 5 % 4 = 1, which holds t5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A table  [7, \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]   block 3  t4 \u003cmark>t5\u003c/mark> t6 a7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Free A: block 7 drops to 1 reference (B still uses it); block 3 drops to 0 and returns to the free list\u003c/p>\u003cpre>refs     block 0: 1   block 1: 1   block 3: \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>   block 7: \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nfree     next out: \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>, 2, 4, ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class BlockPool:\n    \"\"\"Fixed-size KV blocks shared by reference count, as in PagedAttention.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, num_blocks, block_size, order=None):\n        self.block_size = block_size\n        order = list(range(num_blocks)) if order is None else list(order)\n        self.free = order[::-1]                # a stack: pop() hands out order[0] first\n        self.ref = [0] * num_blocks\n        self.slots = [[None] * block_size for _ in range(num_blocks)]  # stands in for K and V\n\n    def alloc(self):\n        if not self.free:\n            raise MemoryError(\"no free KV blocks: preempt a sequence\")\n        b = self.free.pop()\n        self.ref[b] = 1\n        return b\n\n    def release(self, b):\n        self.ref[b] -= 1\n        if self.ref[b] == 0:\n            self.free.append(b)\n\n\nclass Sequence:\n    \"\"\"One sequence's view of the cache: a block table plus a token count.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, pool):\n        self.pool, self.table, self.n = pool, [], 0\n\n    def append(self, kv):\n        pool, slot = self.pool, self.n % self.pool.block_size\n        if slot == 0:                          # no block yet, or the last one is full\n            self.table.append(pool.alloc())\n        elif pool.ref[self.table[-1]] &gt; 1:     # last block is shared: copy on write\n            old, new = self.table[-1], pool.alloc()\n            pool.slots[new][:slot] = pool.slots[old][:slot]\n            pool.release(old)\n            self.table[-1] = new\n        pool.slots[self.table[-1]][slot] = kv\n        self.n += 1\n\n    def fork(self):\n        child = Sequence(self.pool)\n        child.table, child.n = self.table[:], self.n\n        for b in self.table:\n            self.pool.ref[b] += 1\n        return child\n\n    def free(self):\n        for b in self.table:\n            self.pool.release(b)\n        self.table, self.n = [], 0\n\n    def read(self, pos):\n        b, off = divmod(pos, self.pool.block_size)\n        return self.pool.slots[self.table[b]][off]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A random stress test (create, append, fork, free, then compare every \u003ccode>read\u003c/code> with plain Python lists and check that each block's reference count equals the number of tables holding it) passes 300 runs of 300 operations; deleting the copy-on-write branch makes it fail.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Writing into a shared block.\u003c/b> Without copy-on-write, a sample's new token silently overwrites its sibling's cache entry, and the sibling generates from corrupted state with no error.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reference counts that drift.\u003c/b> Forgetting the increment on fork, or freeing twice, returns a block to the free list while a table still maps it: a use-after-free that surfaces as another request's tokens. Assert that counts equal table occurrences in tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off-by-one on allocation.\u003c/b> Allocate when \u003ccode>n % B == 0\u003c/code>, which includes the very first token, not when the block is about to fill.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Leaks on cancellation.\u003c/b> A cancelled, preempted or failed request must free its table, or the pool shrinks until every request is preempted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the indirection cost.\u003c/b> Paging trades a little kernel speed for memory. With fixed, equal lengths you pay that cost for nothing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With B = 16, a sequence holds 40 tokens. How many blocks does it use, where is token 33, and how many slots are wasted?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>ceil(40 / 16) = 3 blocks. Token 33 is in \u003ccode>table[33 // 16] = table[2]\u003c/code> at offset 33 % 16 = 1. The blocks have 48 slots for 40 tokens, so 8 are wasted, all in the last block.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can copy-on-write never need to copy more than one block per forked sequence?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Blocks fill strictly left to right, so every block except the last is full and never written again. A shared full block can stay shared forever. Only the shared, partly filled last block can receive a write, and after it is copied the sequence owns its tail, so later appends either write in place or open fresh blocks.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's \u003ccode>fork\u003c/code> copies the table but does not increment the reference counts. Describe two things that go wrong.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>First, appends never trigger copy-on-write (the count is still 1), so parent and child write into the same last block and corrupt each other. Second, when the parent frees, the counts hit zero and the blocks return to the free list while the child still maps them; the next allocation hands them to another sequence, which overwrites the child's cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A batch classifier runs 512-token inputs and produces exactly one output token each. Is paging worth it here?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Hardly. Every sequence has the same known length, so a contiguous slot per request wastes nothing and avoids the block-table indirection, which the paper measured at 20 to 26% extra attention-kernel latency. Paging pays when lengths vary and grow unpredictably or when sequences share prefixes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Fork a 7-token prompt into four sequences (block size 4), append one token to each, and count blocks in use (pool size minus free blocks). Expected: 5 (one shared full block plus four private copies of the partly filled block); after a second token each, 9; without any sharing, 4 × ceil(9 / 4) = 12.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For lengths [5, 17, 300, 1000, 2047], compute allocated slots for B = 1, 16 and 128, then the fraction used if each request reserved 2,048 contiguous slots. Expected: 3,369, 3,408 and 3,712 slots (waste 0, 39 and 343); contiguous reservation needs 10,240 slots, of which 32.9% are used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the random stress test described under the template, run it, then delete the copy-on-write branch. Expected: the intact version passes every run; the mutant fails an assertion in the first run that forks and appends.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to manage a growing KV cache with a block pool, block tables, reference counts and copy-on-write, and why that is virtual memory again.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:llmserve/how-does-a-radix-tree-let-an-llm-server-reuse-th":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep every cached token sequence in a \u003cb>radix tree\u003c/b>: a trie whose edges carry runs of tokens, where siblings start with different tokens. A new prompt walks down from the root, and the depth it reaches is the number of tokens whose KV cache can be reused, found in time proportional to the prompt length however many sequences are cached. Inserting splits an edge where sequences diverge. When memory runs short, evict \u003cb>leaves\u003c/b> in least-recently-used order, never a node that a running request has \u003cb>locked\u003c/b>, so shared system prompts near the root survive longest.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture every conversation a server has handled written on one branching sheet. The system prompt is the trunk; each conversation branches off where it first differs from the others, and each new turn of a conversation extends its own branch. When a request arrives, you trace it along the sheet as far as it agrees with something already written: that much of its prefill is already done.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That is worth a lot. Prefill costs roughly 2 × parameters floating-point operations per token, so a 1,200-token system prompt re-read by every request of a busy app is a large slice of the server's work. The SGLang paper (Zheng et al. 2024) reports up to 6.4 times higher throughput than other systems on tasks such as agent control, few-shot benchmarks and multi-turn chat, with RadixAttention's KV reuse as one of its main optimisations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>From trie to radix tree\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A trie over token ids would store one node per token. A \u003cb>radix tree\u003c/b> compresses every chain of single-child nodes into one edge labelled with a run of tokens. When every inner node is a branching point, N stored sequences need fewer than 2N nodes however long they are (splits made while matching, below, can add a few single-child nodes). Children are keyed by the first token of their edge, which is unique among siblings, so choosing the next edge is one dictionary lookup.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>root\n └─ [SYS be]                        shared by every request of this app\n     ├─ [kind]\n     │   ├─ [Q1 hello]\n     │   │   └─ [A1 hi Q3 bye]      conversation 1, turn 2\n     │   └─ [Q2 weather]            conversation 2\n     └─ [terse Q4 joke]             a variant of the system prompt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each node also stores the KV cache for the tokens on its edge (in SGLang, indices into a token-granular pool: the paper uses a page size of one token).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Match, splitting as you go\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Walk from the root. At each node, look up the child whose edge starts with the next prompt token, then compare tokens along that edge. If the whole edge matches, descend and continue. If it matches only partly, \u003cb>split\u003c/b> the edge at the mismatch, so the matched part becomes a node of its own, and stop. The matched length is the reusable prefix, and the node where the walk ends marks exactly the reused part, which you need for locking.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Insert\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Match, then hang the unmatched remainder of the sequence as one new leaf under the node where the match stopped. Servers insert the prompt and the generated output, so the next turn of the same conversation matches all of it.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Locks and least-recently-used eviction\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Every node keeps a \u003cb>lock count\u003c/b>: the number of running requests whose prefix passes through it. Starting a request adds one to every node from its end node up to the root; finishing subtracts one. Every node also keeps the time it was last on a matched path.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To free memory, collect the unlocked leaves into a min-heap keyed by last access time, pop the oldest, delete it, and if its parent has just become an unlocked leaf, push the parent too. Evicting only leaves keeps the tree consistent: a token's key and value depend on every token before it, so an inner node's cache is useless without its ancestors, and a subtree under a deleted node could never be reached again. Since a parent is touched whenever any of its children is, it is never older than its most recent child, so leaves-first agrees with plain LRU.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Scheduling for hits\u003c/h5>\u003cp>SGLang defines the \u003cb>cache hit rate\u003c/b> as cached prompt tokens divided by prompt tokens. The order of waiting requests matters: alternating between unrelated prompts thrashes the cache. Its longest-shared-prefix-first policy is a depth-first walk of the radix tree of waiting requests, and Theorem 3.1 of the paper shows that order achieves the optimal hit rate for a batch when the cache holds at least the longest request.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The other design: chained block hashes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>vLLM finds reusable prefixes without a tree. Each \u003cb>full\u003c/b> block of B tokens gets a hash of its parent block's hash and its own tokens, so one hash identifies the whole prefix up to that block; a new request hashes its prompt block by block and looks each hash up in a dictionary until the first miss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Radix tree (SGLang)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Chained block hashes (vLLM)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reuse granularity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>any token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>whole blocks only (a partial last block is never reused)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lookup\u003c/td>\u003ctd>walk the tree: O(L) token comparisons\u003c/td>\u003ctd>hash each block: O(L) hashing, O(L/B) dictionary lookups\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Eviction unit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a whole leaf, of any length\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one block, least recently used first\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Structure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pointers and splits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>flat dictionary and append-only block tables\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let L be the prompt length. \u003cb>Match\u003c/b> consumes at least one prompt token per child lookup and compares each prompt token at most once, so it is O(L), independent of how many sequences are cached; the brute-force alternative compares the prompt with every cached sequence, O(N × L). A split slices one edge, O(edge length) in Python. \u003cb>Insert\u003c/b> is O(L). \u003cb>Lock and unlock\u003c/b> are O(depth). The template's \u003cb>evict\u003c/b> rescans the tree for unlocked leaves, O(nodes), then pops E leaves from the heap, O(E log E); SGLang keeps a running set of evictable leaves instead of rescanning. The tree's own memory (a few pointers and integers per node) is negligible next to 128 KiB of KV per token.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use a prefix cache when prompts share long beginnings: one system prompt per app, few-shot examples, documents queried repeatedly, multi-turn chat (turn n re-sends turns 1 to n − 1), agent loops, several samples of one prompt. Prefer a radix tree when sharing is deep and branchy and you want token-level reuse; chained block hashes are simpler and fit a block allocator directly. Do not expect gains when prompts are unique, and remember that prefix caching only shortens prefill: it does nothing for long generations. In multi-tenant servers, keep tenants' caches apart (vLLM supports a per-request cache salt), because hit timing can leak whether someone else sent a prompt.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Tokens are written as words. Times count operations; each node shows its last access time t.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert R1 \"SYS be kind Q1 hello\" into an empty tree: no match, one leaf with five tokens\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[SYS be kind Q1 hello]  t=1\ncached 5 tokens; hit 0 of 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert R2 \"SYS be kind Q2 weather\": the edge matches 3 tokens, then Q2 differs from Q1, so split\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>[SYS be kind]  t=2\u003c/mark>\n    [Q1 hello]  t=1\n    \u003cmark>[Q2 weather]  t=2\u003c/mark>\ncached 7; hit 3 of 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert R3, conversation 1's second turn \"SYS be kind Q1 hello A1 hi Q3 bye\": 5 tokens match, append a leaf\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[SYS be kind]  t=3\n    [Q1 hello]  t=3\n        \u003cmark>[A1 hi Q3 bye]  t=3\u003c/mark>\n    [Q2 weather]  t=2\ncached 11; hit 5 of 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert R4 \"SYS be terse Q4 joke\": only \"SYS be\" matches, so the top edge splits again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>[SYS be]  t=4\u003c/mark>\n    \u003cmark>[kind]  t=3\u003c/mark>\n        [Q1 hello]  t=3\n            [A1 hi Q3 bye]  t=3\n        [Q2 weather]  t=2\n    \u003cmark>[terse Q4 joke]  t=4\u003c/mark>\ncached 14; hit 2 of 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Evict at least 4 tokens. Unlocked leaves by age: Q2 weather (t=2), A1 hi Q3 bye (t=3), terse Q4 joke (t=4)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop \u003cmark>[Q2 weather]\u003c/mark>: frees 2; its parent [kind] still has a child\npop \u003cmark>[A1 hi Q3 bye]\u003c/mark>: frees 4; its parent [Q1 hello] becomes a leaf and joins the heap\nfreed 6 &gt;= 4: stop\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">After eviction: the shared system prompt survives; R2 asked again would reuse 3 tokens\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[SYS be]  t=4\n    [kind]  t=3\n        [Q1 hello]  t=3\n    [terse Q4 joke]  t=4\ncached 8 tokens\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\nimport itertools\n\n\nclass Node:\n    __slots__ = (\"children\", \"parent\", \"key\", \"lock\", \"last\", \"id\")\n    ids = itertools.count()\n\n    def __init__(self, parent=None, key=()):\n        self.children = {}          # first token of the child's edge -&gt; child\n        self.parent = parent\n        self.key = key              # the run of tokens on the edge into this node\n        self.lock = 0               # running requests whose prefix passes through here\n        self.last = 0               # logical time of the last access\n        self.id = next(Node.ids)\n\n\nclass RadixCache:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = Node()\n        self.root.lock = 1          # the root is never evicted\n        self.clock = itertools.count(1)\n        self.size = 0               # cached tokens\n\n    def _split(self, child, k):\n        \"\"\"Cut child's edge after k tokens; return the new node for the first part.\"\"\"\n        mid = Node(child.parent, child.key[:k])\n        mid.lock, mid.last = child.lock, child.last\n        child.parent.children[mid.key[0]] = mid\n        child.key, child.parent = child.key[k:], mid\n        mid.children[child.key[0]] = child\n        return mid\n\n    def match(self, tokens):\n        \"\"\"Longest cached prefix of tokens: (its length, the node where it ends).\"\"\"\n        now, node, i = next(self.clock), self.root, 0\n        node.last = now\n        while i &lt; len(tokens) and tokens[i] in node.children:\n            child = node.children[tokens[i]]\n            k = 0\n            while k &lt; len(child.key) and i + k &lt; len(tokens) and child.key[k] == tokens[i + k]:\n                k += 1\n            if k &lt; len(child.key):\n                child = self._split(child, k)\n            child.last = now\n            node, i = child, i + k\n        return i, node\n\n    def insert(self, tokens):\n        \"\"\"Cache tokens; return (tokens that were already cached, node at the end).\"\"\"\n        hit, node = self.match(tokens)\n        if hit &lt; len(tokens):\n            leaf = Node(node, tuple(tokens[hit:]))\n            leaf.last = node.last\n            node.children[leaf.key[0]] = leaf\n            self.size += len(leaf.key)\n            node = leaf\n        return hit, node\n\n    def lock(self, node, delta):\n        \"\"\"delta=+1 pins the path root..node while a request runs; -1 unpins it.\"\"\"\n        while node is not self.root:\n            node.lock += delta\n            node = node.parent\n\n    def evict(self, n):\n        \"\"\"Free at least n tokens, least recently used unlocked leaves first.\"\"\"\n        heap = []\n        stack = [self.root]\n        while stack:\n            x = stack.pop()\n            stack.extend(x.children.values())\n            if not x.children and x.lock == 0:\n                heap.append((x.last, x.id, x))\n        heapq.heapify(heap)\n        freed = 0\n        while freed &lt; n and heap:\n            _, _, x = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            del x.parent.children[x.key[0]]\n            freed += len(x.key)\n            p = x.parent\n            if not p.children and p.lock == 0:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (p.last, p.id, p))\n        self.size -= freed\n        return freed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tested against brute force: \u003ccode>match\u003c/code> equals the longest common prefix with any cached root-to-leaf sequence (500 random runs with inserts, locks and evictions), \u003ccode>evict\u003c/code> frees exactly what a rescan-every-time reference frees, and locked paths always survive.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matching without splitting.\u003c/b> If a match can end in the middle of an edge, there is no node that marks the reused part, so you cannot lock exactly it and cannot hang the new suffix in the right place.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evicting inner nodes.\u003c/b> Deleting a node strands its subtree: memory that can never be matched again. Evict leaves and let parents become leaves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting locks.\u003c/b> Evicting a node that a running request is reading frees KV memory still in use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Updating only the end node's time.\u003c/b> Every node on the matched path was used; if ancestors keep old times, LRU order is wrong.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Text instead of token ids.\u003c/b> The tree is keyed by token ids. A changed chat template, a timestamp near the top or a different tokenisation of the same text misses from that point on.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Coarse eviction.\u003c/b> A leaf goes as a whole, even if it is a 3,000-token conversation. With a small cache, a block-granular LRU can keep more useful heads than a leaf-granular one (the lab measures this).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">After the trace's four inserts (before the eviction), what does \u003ccode>match\u003c/code> return for \"SYS be terse Q5 pun\" and for \"SYS be kind Q1 hello A1 hi Q9\", and what happens to the tree?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>3 and 7. The first walks [SYS be] then matches only \"terse\" on [terse Q4 joke], splitting it into [terse] and [Q4 joke]. The second walks [SYS be], [kind], [Q1 hello], then matches \"A1 hi\" on [A1 hi Q3 bye], splitting it into [A1 hi] and [Q3 bye]. The tree now has 9 nodes including the root and still caches 14 tokens: matching splits nodes but never adds or removes tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the cache evict leaves first instead of the least recently used node anywhere in the tree?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node's key and value tensors were computed with all earlier tokens in context, so a node is only useful together with its ancestors, and a subtree below a deleted node can never be reached by a match again. Evicting leaves keeps the cache a set of complete prefixes. It also agrees with LRU, because every match that touches a child touches its parent too, so a parent is never older than its newest child.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A 1,000-token prompt equals a cached one except for its last token. How many tokens does a radix tree reuse, and how many does a chained block hash with B = 16 reuse?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The radix tree reuses 999 tokens. The block cache reuses only full matching blocks: the first 62 blocks cover tokens 0 to 991 and match, while the last 8 tokens form a partial block, which is never cached, so it reuses 992.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An implementation keys each node's children by the whole edge label instead of its first token. What breaks?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>To continue a match you know only the next prompt token, not the length or content of the edge that should follow, so you cannot look the child up; you would have to scan all children. Keying by the first token works because siblings always start with different tokens, which the split operation maintains.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Rebuild the trace, then run the two matches from the first question. Expected: 3 and 7, a tree of 9 nodes including the root, and \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> still 14.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Continue from that state: match the full second turn of conversation 1 (\"SYS be kind Q1 hello A1 hi Q3 bye\"), lock the returned node, and call \u003ccode>evict(100)\u003c/code>. Expected: 5 tokens freed ([Q2 weather], then [Q4 joke], then its parent [terse] once it becomes a leaf) and 9 tokens left, all on the locked path. Unlock and call \u003ccode>evict(100)\u003c/code> again: 9 more freed, size 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a brute-force checker that collects all root-to-leaf sequences and computes the longest common prefix with a query; compare it with \u003ccode>match\u003c/code> after random inserts and evictions. Expected: no mismatch in a few hundred random runs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to cache and reuse KV across requests with a radix tree, lock the paths in use, and evict least recently used leaves.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:llmserve/how-does-beam-search-keep-the-k-best-hypotheses-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Greedy decoding commits to the single most likely token at every step and can miss a sequence that is more likely overall. \u003cb>Beam search\u003c/b> keeps the k best partial sequences: at each step it extends every beam by every token, scores the candidates by summed log-probability and keeps the best k with a \u003cb>size-k min-heap\u003c/b>. Finished hypotheses leave the beam. Each token adds a negative log-probability, so raw scores favour short outputs; comparing \u003cb>log P / length^α\u003c/b> corrects for that.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You are choosing a route through a city with a map that only rates the next street. Taking the best-rated street every time (greedy) can lead you into a district with only bad exits. Keeping your two or three best partial routes open, and extending each by one street at a time, lets a route with a mediocre first street win if what follows is excellent.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A toy model shows it. From the start, \"the\" has probability 0.5 and \"a\" 0.4. After \"the\", the end-of-sequence token has 0.4; after \"a\", \"cat\" has 0.9. Greedy takes \"the\" and ends with probability 0.5 × 0.4 = 0.2. The sequence \"a cat &lt;eos&gt;\" has 0.4 × 0.9 × 0.6 = 0.216: more likely, but invisible to greedy.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>State\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A beam is a pair (log-probability, tokens). Sum log-probabilities instead of multiplying probabilities: the product of hundreds of probabilities underflows to 0.0 in floating point, while the sum of their logarithms stays well scaled.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>One step\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For every live beam and every vocabulary token, form the candidate (log P + log p(token), tokens + token). Keep the k best of these k × V candidates. A \u003cb>size-k min-heap\u003c/b> does that in one pass: push until it holds k items; afterwards, if a candidate beats the heap's minimum, replace the minimum (\u003ccode>heapq.heapreplace\u003c/code>). The heap always holds the k best seen so far, and its root is the weakest of them, the one to beat.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A useful shortcut: at most k survivors can come from one beam, and they must be among that beam's own k best tokens. So real implementations take each beam's top k first (a partial selection over V) and then select the k best of k × k.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Finished hypotheses\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A candidate ending in the end-of-sequence token goes to a finished list instead of the next beam, so the live beam shrinks. In the template, the search stops when no live beam remains or the length limit is reached. Libraries differ in how they refill the beam and when they stop early, so read their documentation before comparing outputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Length normalisation\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Every extra token adds a negative log-probability, so with raw scores a long hypothesis must be much more probable per token to beat a short one. Google's translation system (Wu et al. 2016, §7) first divided by the length, then by length^α with α between 0.6 and 0.7, and finally used lp(Y) = ((5 + |Y|) / 6)^α. Hugging Face's \u003ccode>length_penalty\u003c/code> divides the summed log-probability by length raised to that power, so values above 0 favour longer outputs. With α = 0 nothing changes; with α = 1 you compare average log-probability per token.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, a 2-token hypothesis with log P = −2.0 beats a 6-token one with −3.0 on raw score. At α = 0.6 their scores are −1.320 and −1.024, and at α = 1 they are −1.0 and −0.5: the longer one wins.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Sharing memory between beams\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Beams share their prefix. PagedAttention (§4.4) maps the shared blocks into every beam's table with reference counts; when a beam drops out, its blocks' counts fall and blocks reaching zero are freed, and only the last, partly filled block is ever copied.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Per step, scoring k × V candidates with the heap costs O(kV log k) comparisons; with the per-beam shortcut and a linear-time partial selection it is O(kV + k² log k). Over T steps: O(T × kV log k) for selection, plus the model work, which dominates: one forward pass per beam per step, usually batched as one pass of batch size k. Memory is k sequences of up to T tokens, much less with shared KV blocks. Exhaustive search would score V^T sequences: with V = 50,000 and T = 20 that is about 10^94.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Decoder\u003c/th>\u003cth>Deterministic\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weak at\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Greedy (k = 1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>cheap baselines, short extractions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>misses better sequences, loops\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Beam search (k = 4 to 12)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>input-grounded tasks: translation, speech recognition, captioning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>open-ended text: bland and repetitive\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sampling with top-k or top-p\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no\u003c/td>\u003ctd>chat, creative and open-ended text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>exact reproducibility\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The nucleus sampling paper (Holtzman et al. 2020) shows that maximising probability in open-ended generation produces degenerate, repetitive text, which is why chat models sample. Wider beams give diminishing returns: Google's translation system kept 8 to 12 hypotheses and found 4 or 2 only slightly worse.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The toy model above, k = 2. Probabilities are shown instead of log-probabilities for readability; the heap stores (log P, tokens) and compares log P.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 1 from the start: candidates the 0.5, a 0.4, &lt;eos&gt; 0.1. Keep the two best\u003c/p>\u003cpre>beams    \u003cmark>the 0.5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>a 0.4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 2, scanning beam \"the\": \"the cat\" 0.15 and \"the dog\" 0.15 fill the size-2 heap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>heap (min first)   the cat 0.15 | the dog 0.15\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\"the &lt;eos&gt;\" 0.20 beats the root 0.15: heapreplace drops \"the cat\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>heap (min first)   the dog 0.15 | \u003cmark>the &lt;eos&gt; 0.20\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Scanning beam \"a\": \"a cat\" 0.36 beats 0.15, replacing \"the dog\"; \"a &lt;eos&gt;\" 0.04 does not beat 0.20\u003c/p>\u003cpre>heap (min first)   the &lt;eos&gt; 0.20 | \u003cmark>a cat 0.36\u003c/mark>\nfinished: the &lt;eos&gt; 0.20      live: a cat\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 3: one live beam gives \"a cat &lt;eos&gt;\" 0.216 and \"a cat sat\" 0.144; both fit in the heap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finished: the &lt;eos&gt; 0.20, \u003cmark>a cat &lt;eos&gt; 0.216\u003c/mark>      live: a cat sat\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 4: \"a cat sat &lt;eos&gt;\" 0.144 finishes; no live beam remains, so stop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finished: the &lt;eos&gt; 0.200, a cat &lt;eos&gt; 0.216, \u003cmark>a cat sat &lt;eos&gt; 0.144\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rank the finished hypotheses by log P / length^alpha\u003c/p>\u003cpre>hypothesis        len   alpha 0    alpha 0.6   alpha 1\nthe &lt;eos&gt;          2    -1.609     -1.062      -0.805\na cat &lt;eos&gt;        3    \u003cmark>-1.532\u003c/mark>     \u003cmark>-0.793\u003c/mark>      -0.511\na cat sat &lt;eos&gt;    4    -1.938     -0.844      \u003cmark>-0.484\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Beam search beats greedy (0.216 against 0.2), and the length penalty decides between the two \"a cat\" hypotheses.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef beam_search(next_logprobs, k, max_len, eos, alpha=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Best hypothesis under score = log P / len**alpha, keeping k live beams.\n\n    next_logprobs(tokens) returns {token: log probability} for the next token.\n    \"\"\"\n    beams = [(0.0, ())]                          # (cumulative log prob, tokens)\n    finished = []                                # (score, log prob, tokens)\n    for _ in range(max_len):\n        best = []                                # min-heap holding the k best extensions\n        for logp, toks in beams:\n            for tok, lp in next_logprobs(toks).items():\n                item = (logp + lp, toks + (tok,))\n                if len(best) &lt; k:\n                    heapq.heappush(best, item)\n                elif item &gt; best[0]:\n                    heapq.heapreplace(best, item)    # evict the weakest of the k\n        beams = []\n        for logp, toks in best:\n            if toks[-1] == eos:\n                finished.append((logp / len(toks) ** alpha, logp, toks))\n            else:\n                beams.append((logp, toks))\n        if not beams:\n            break\n    pool = finished or [(lp / len(t) ** alpha, lp, t) for lp, t in beams]\n    return max(pool)\n\n\ndef greedy(next_logprobs, max_len, eos):\n    toks, logp = (), 0.0\n    for _ in range(max_len):\n        tok, lp = max(next_logprobs(toks).items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])\n        toks, logp = toks + (tok,), logp + lp\n        if tok == eos:\n            break\n    return logp, toks\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tested on 150 random toy models: with k at least V^4 the result equals exhaustive search over all sequences up to length 4 for α = 0, 0.6 and 1, and with k = 1 it equals \u003ccode>greedy\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Raw scores across lengths.\u003c/b> Without normalisation the search prefers stopping early; decide α on purpose and report it with results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Multiplying probabilities.\u003c/b> Products underflow to 0.0 for long outputs, after which every hypothesis ties. Add log-probabilities.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keeping finished hypotheses in the beam.\u003c/b> Extending a hypothesis past its end token wastes beam slots and can produce text after the end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ties.\u003c/b> Equal scores are broken by whatever comparison follows (here the token tuples): deterministic, but arbitrary. Fix the rule if outputs must be reproducible across implementations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assuming a wider beam is better.\u003c/b> More search finds higher-probability text, which for open-ended prompts is often duller text.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run the template on the toy model with k = 1. What does it return, and why can it never find \"a cat &lt;eos&gt;\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\"the &lt;eos&gt;\" with probability 0.2, the same as greedy. With one beam, step 1 keeps only \"the\" (0.5 beats 0.4), and every later candidate extends \"the\", so no hypothesis starting with \"a\" is ever formed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it safe to take each beam's own top k tokens before selecting the k best overall?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If a beam contributed a candidate that is not among its own k best tokens, then that beam's k better candidates would all outrank it, so the global top k would already hold at least k candidates better than it, a contradiction. Every survivor is therefore in its beam's top k, and selecting from k × k candidates gives the same answer as selecting from k × V.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You are building an English-to-French translator and a story-writing assistant. Which decoder do you choose for each?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Beam search (with a modest k and a tuned length penalty) for translation, where the output is tightly determined by the input and the most probable output is usually a good one. Sampling with top-p or top-k for stories: maximising probability on open-ended text gives repetitive, generic prose, as the nucleus sampling paper shows.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague scores hypotheses with \u003ccode>math.prod(probs)\u003c/code> and sees all beams tie at 0.0 on long outputs. What is wrong and what is the fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Multiplying hundreds of probabilities below one underflows below the smallest positive float, so every score becomes 0.0 and comparisons are meaningless. Sum log-probabilities instead; length normalisation is then a division of that sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>beam_search\u003c/code> on the toy model (use the table in this card) with k = 1, 2 and 3 and α = 0 and 1. Expected: k = 1 returns \"the &lt;eos&gt;\" (P = 0.200) for both α; k = 2 and k = 3 return \"a cat &lt;eos&gt;\" (P = 0.216) with α = 0 and \"a cat sat &lt;eos&gt;\" (P = 0.144) with α = 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write an exhaustive search over all sequences up to length 4 for random models with 2 to 4 tokens, and compare it with \u003ccode>beam_search\u003c/code> at k = V^4. Expected: identical best hypotheses and scores for every model and every α.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the per-beam top-k shortcut with \u003ccode>heapq.nlargest(k, ...)\u003c/code> on each beam's distribution before the global selection. Expected: identical results on all your random models, with far fewer heap operations when V is large.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to run beam search with a size-k heap and decide how strongly to normalise by length.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:llmserve/how-do-top-k-and-top-p-sampling-choose-the-next-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sampling turns V logits into one token. Divide the logits by the \u003cb>temperature\u003c/b> and apply a softmax, subtracting the maximum first; \u003cb>top-k\u003c/b> keeps the k most likely tokens, found with a size-k heap or a linear-time partition; \u003cb>top-p\u003c/b> keeps the smallest set of most likely tokens whose probabilities reach p, found by sorting, taking \u003cb>prefix sums\u003c/b> and \u003cb>binary searching\u003c/b> for p. The draw itself is one more binary search: take u uniform in [0, 1) and find where u × total falls among the cumulative sums.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Give every token raffle tickets in proportion to its probability and lay them end to end along a line one metre long. Throwing a dart at a uniformly random point on the line picks a token with exactly its probability. Temperature reshapes the tickets: below 1 the favourite's share grows, above 1 the shares even out. Top-k and top-p tear the tail off the line, the many unlikely tokens whose combined share would otherwise produce occasional nonsense, and stretch what is left back to one metre.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Temperature and a stable softmax\u003c/h5>\u003cp>p_i = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T). Logits can be large, and \u003ccode>math.exp(1000)\u003c/code> overflows, so subtract the largest scaled logit before exponentiating: the result is unchanged because the factor cancels, and every exponent is now at most 0. T = 0 means \"always the top token\" and must be special-cased as argmax, since dividing by zero is undefined.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Top-k\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Keep the k largest probabilities. Three ways:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>heapq.nlargest(k, ids, key=...)\u003c/code>: one pass with a size-k heap, O(V log k).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>numpy.argpartition\u003c/code>: a selection algorithm (introselect) that places the k largest in front in O(V) on average, unordered.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorting everything: O(V log V), simplest when you need the order anyway.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Top-p (the nucleus)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The nucleus is the smallest set whose total probability is at least p (Holtzman et al. 2020, §3.1). Sort ids by probability, descending. Among all sets of a given size, the top prefix of that order has the most mass, so the smallest set reaching p is a prefix: compute prefix sums \u003ccode>cum\u003c/code> and the answer is the first index where \u003ccode>cum[i] &gt;= p\u003c/code>. Because probabilities are non-negative, \u003ccode>cum\u003c/code> is sorted, so \u003ccode>bisect_left(cum, p)\u003c/code> finds that index in O(log V). Keep \u003ccode>m = index + 1\u003c/code> tokens. The nucleus adapts: when the model is confident it holds one or two tokens; when unsure, hundreds.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Drawing: inverse-CDF sampling\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Build prefix sums over the kept ids, take \u003ccode>target = u * cum[-1]\u003c/code> (this renormalises without dividing every probability) and return the id at \u003ccode>bisect_right(cum, target)\u003c/code>: the first prefix that exceeds the target. Each id owns an interval of the line as long as its probability.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Order of operations\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Hugging Face Transformers (v5.22) applies temperature, then top-k, then top-p, then min-p; each stage works on the survivors of the previous one, renormalised. Its top-p sorts the whole vocabulary, takes the cumulative softmax and masks the tail. The template below follows the same order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For V = 128,000 and k = 50:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Step\u003c/th>\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Temperature and softmax\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one pass for the max, one for the sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top-k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>size-k heap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V log k)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top-k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>argpartition\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V) average\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top-p over the whole vocabulary\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort, prefix sums, bisect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V log V) + O(V) + O(log V)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top-p after top-k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sort only the k survivors\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(k log k)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Draw\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix sums, bisect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(m) + O(log m) for m kept ids\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Running top-k first shrinks top-p's sort from V to k items. If you need many draws from one distribution (several samples of one prompt), build the prefix sums once and pay O(log m) per draw.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sample when you want varied, natural text: chat, writing, brainstorming, several candidate answers to vote on. Top-p adapts to the model's confidence, which a fixed k cannot (Holtzman et al. §3.2: a flat distribution needs a large k, a peaked one a small k). Top-k is the cheaper cut and a guard against a long tail when the distribution is flat. Do not sample when you need exactly reproducible output, such as extraction into fixed fields; use greedy decoding there, and remember the AI guide's determinism lesson: even temperature 0 is not bit-reproducible on a busy server.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Six tokens: sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, foggy, purple with logits 3.0, 2.2, 1.5, 1.0, 0.2, −2.0. Temperature 0.7, p = 0.9, u = 0.73.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Divide by T = 0.7, subtract the max (3.0 / 0.7), exponentiate and normalise\u003c/p>\u003cpre>token   sunny  cloudy  rainy  windy  foggy  purple\nprob    0.661  0.211   0.078  0.038  0.012  0.001\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort ids by probability, descending (already in order here), and take prefix sums\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sorted  sunny  cloudy  rainy  windy  foggy  purple\ncum     0.661  0.872   0.949  0.987  0.999  1.000\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">bisect_left(cum, 0.9) = 2: the first prefix reaching 0.9 ends at index 2, so keep m = 3 tokens\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cum     0.661  0.872  \u003cmark>0.949\u003c/mark>  0.987  0.999  1.000\nnucleus \u003cmark>sunny  cloudy  rainy\u003c/mark>   mass 0.949\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Draw: prefix sums over the nucleus; target = u x total = 0.73 x 0.949 = 0.693\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cum     0.661  0.872  0.949\ntarget  0.693\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">bisect_right(cum, 0.693) = 1: the first prefix exceeding the target is cloudy's\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cum     0.661 \u003cmark>0.872\u003c/mark> 0.949\nsample  \u003cmark>cloudy\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every u below 0.661 / 0.949 = 0.697 gives sunny, u up to 0.919 gives cloudy, and the rest gives rainy: each token's share of the nucleus.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import bisect\nimport heapq\nimport itertools\nimport math\n\n\ndef softmax(logits, temperature=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Probabilities from logits divided by temperature (&gt; 0).\"\"\"\n    z = [x / temperature for x in logits]\n    m = max(z)                                   # subtract the max: exp never overflows\n    e = [math.exp(x - m) for x in z]\n    s = sum(e)\n    return [x / s for x in e]\n\n\ndef top_k(probs, k):\n    \"\"\"Ids of the k most likely tokens: O(V log k) with a size-k heap.\"\"\"\n    return heapq.nlargest(k, range(len(probs)), key=probs.__getitem__)\n\n\ndef top_p(probs, p):\n    \"\"\"Smallest set of most likely ids whose mass reaches p (the nucleus).\"\"\"\n    order = sorted(range(len(probs)), key=probs.__getitem__, reverse=True)\n    cum = list(itertools.accumulate(probs[i] for i in order))\n    m = bisect.bisect_left(cum, p) + 1           # first prefix whose sum is &gt;= p\n    return order[:min(m, len(order))]\n\n\ndef draw(probs, ids, u):\n    \"\"\"Inverse-CDF draw among ids, renormalised; u is uniform in [0, 1).\"\"\"\n    cum = list(itertools.accumulate(probs[i] for i in ids))\n    j = bisect.bisect_right(cum, u * cum[-1])\n    return ids[min(j, len(ids) - 1)]             # guard against rounding at u near 1\n\n\ndef sample(logits, u, temperature=1.0, k=None, p=None):\n    \"\"\"Temperature, then top-k, then top-p on the renormalised survivors, then draw.\"\"\"\n    probs = softmax(logits, temperature)\n    ids = top_k(probs, k) if k else list(range(len(probs)))\n    if p is not None:\n        total = sum(probs[i] for i in ids)\n        keep = top_p([probs[i] / total for i in ids], p)\n        ids = [ids[j] for j in keep]\n    return draw(probs, ids, u)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In real use, pass \u003ccode>u = random.random()\u003c/code>. Tested: \u003ccode>top_p\u003c/code> against brute force over all subsets (3,000 random cases with V up to 7), \u003ccode>draw\u003c/code> against exact shares on a grid of 20,000 values of u, and \u003ccode>sample\u003c/code> against a NumPy version built on \u003ccode>argpartition\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cumsum\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>searchsorted\u003c/code> (3,000 random cases).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>exp\u003c/code> of a large logit overflows; always subtract the maximum first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Temperature 0.\u003c/b> Dividing by zero; treat it as argmax.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cutting unsorted probabilities.\u003c/b> Prefix sums only find the nucleus after sorting by probability.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Losing the ids.\u003c/b> Sorting probabilities without carrying their ids along (sort ids by key, as the template does) returns positions in the sorted list, not tokens.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ties at the boundary.\u003c/b> If two tokens share the boundary probability, the nucleus is not unique; implementations keep whichever their sort puts first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rounding at u close to 1.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>u * cum[-1]\u003c/code> can round up to \u003ccode>cum[-1]\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code> then returns one past the end; clamp the index.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">At T = 1.0 the six probabilities are 0.533, 0.240, 0.119, 0.072, 0.032 and 0.004. Which tokens does top-p = 0.9 keep?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The prefix sums are 0.533, 0.773, 0.892, 0.964, ...; \u003ccode>bisect_left(cum, 0.9)\u003c/code> is 3, so four tokens: sunny, cloudy, rainy and windy (0.892 after three tokens is still short of 0.9).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the first prefix of the sorted order that reaches p give the smallest possible set?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For any size s, the s most likely tokens have at least as much mass as any other set of s tokens. If the first prefix reaching p has m tokens, the prefix of m − 1 tokens falls short of p, so every set of m − 1 or fewer tokens falls short too. No smaller set can reach p.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With V = 128,000 and k = 50, how would you compute top-k in pure Python and in NumPy?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In pure Python, \u003ccode>heapq.nlargest\u003c/code> with a size-50 heap: O(V log k), one pass, no full sort. In NumPy, \u003ccode>argpartition\u003c/code> selects the 50 largest in O(V) in compiled code (then sort those 50 if you need them ordered). Both avoid the O(V log V) full sort.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague writes \u003ccode>cum = list(accumulate(sorted(probs, reverse=True)))\u003c/code> and returns \u003ccode>bisect_right(cum, u)\u003c/code> as the sampled token id. Name two bugs.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The index refers to a position in the sorted list, not a token id, because the ids were not sorted along with the probabilities. And \u003ccode>u\u003c/code> was not scaled by the kept mass, so after truncation (where \u003ccode>cum[-1]\u003c/code> is below 1) values of u above it fall off the end.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute the nucleus for p = 0.9 at temperatures 0.3, 0.7, 1.0 and 2.0 for the six-token example. Expected: 1, 3, 4 and 5 tokens (sunny alone at 0.3; foggy joins at 2.0).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Call \u003ccode>sample(logits, u, temperature=1.0, p=0.9)\u003c/code> for u = 0.05, 0.5 and 0.95. Expected: sunny, sunny, windy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Call \u003ccode>sample(logits, 0.73, temperature=0.7, k=3, p=0.9)\u003c/code> and explain the result. Expected: sunny; after top-3 the survivors renormalise to 0.696, 0.222 and 0.082, so the nucleus is only sunny and cloudy, and the target 0.73 × 0.872 = 0.637 falls in sunny's interval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write a brute-force nucleus (try every subset, smallest size first) and compare it with \u003ccode>top_p\u003c/code> on random distributions of up to 7 tokens. Expected: the same size and the same mass every time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to apply temperature, top-k and top-p with heaps, prefix sums and binary search, and what each step costs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:llmserve/how-does-continuous-batching-decide-what-runs-at":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A server runs the model one step at a time over a batch, and \u003cb>continuous batching\u003c/b> (Orca's iteration-level scheduling) rebuilds that batch at every step. Underneath are four structures: a FIFO \u003cb>waiting queue\u003c/b>, a \u003cb>running list\u003c/b> in admission order, a per-step \u003cb>token budget\u003c/b> shared by decode tokens and prefill chunks, and a pool of KV \u003cb>blocks\u003c/b>. Running requests are planned first, then waiting ones are admitted in order while budget and blocks last. When a running request cannot get a block, the newest running request is \u003cb>preempted\u003c/b>: its blocks are freed and it returns to the front of the queue, to be recomputed later.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A city bus does not wait at the depot until forty passengers have booked and then drive them all to the end of the line. It stops at every stop: whoever has arrived gets off, whoever is waiting gets on if there is a seat. Static batching is the depot bus: a batch runs until its slowest member finishes, and early finishers leave empty seats. Continuous batching is the city bus, re-planned at every step. The seats are KV memory and the bus's speed limit is the per-step token budget.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>One counter per request\u003c/h5>\u003cp>vLLM's V1 scheduler has no separate prefill and decode phases. Each request has a number of tokens whose KV is already computed, and each step tries to let that number catch up with the request's current length (prompt plus output so far). A new request needs its whole prompt computed, possibly in several chunks (chunked prefill); a decoding request needs one token (the one it just produced). When a request's computed count reaches its length, the step emits its next token.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The step loop\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Running requests first\u003c/b>, in admission order: give each \u003ccode>min(need, budget left)\u003c/code> tokens, growing its block table if the new tokens cross a block boundary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Out of blocks?\u003c/b> Preempt the newest running request: free all its blocks, reset its computed count to zero, put it back at the front of the waiting queue. Repeat until the current request fits, or until the request preempted is the current one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Admit waiting requests\u003c/b>, first come first served, while there is budget, a free sequence slot and blocks for the first chunk. Stop at the first request that does not fit, so later arrivals cannot overtake it. If this step preempted anything, admit nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Run the step\u003c/b>, emit tokens, and retire finished requests, returning their blocks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>Why newest first, all or nothing\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The PagedAttention paper serves requests first come first served and preempts the latest arrivals first, so the earliest requests always make progress. It evicts all of a sequence's blocks or none, because a sequence needs all of its blocks at every step. Recovery is by swapping blocks to CPU memory or by recomputation; recomputation is cheaper than it sounds, because the prompt and the tokens already generated are reprocessed as one parallel prefill. vLLM's current engine recomputes, and its V1 scheduler admits no waiting request in a step that preempted, which avoids thrashing.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Priorities and the alternative\u003c/h5>\u003cp>vLLM also offers priority scheduling: the waiting queue becomes a heap (Python \u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code>) and the victim is the running request with the largest (priority, arrival time). Orca, the original iteration-level scheduler, avoided preemption by reserving each request's maximum KV size when it was first scheduled. That is safe but over-reserves, the waste PagedAttention removed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Per step the scheduler touches each running request once and each admitted request once: O(R + A), with O(1) deque operations, or O(log W) per heap operation in priority mode. It runs on the CPU between GPU steps of a few milliseconds to tens of milliseconds, so it must stay this simple.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The payoff, from a seeded simulation of 200 requests (prompts of 20 to 200 tokens, outputs of 5 to 120, at most 16 running):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Scheduler\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mean latency (steps)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Makespan (steps)\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Static batches of 16 (whole prompt in one step)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>670.4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,572\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Continuous, unlimited budget and memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>320.1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>901\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Continuous, 512 tokens per step, 120 blocks of 16\u003c/td>\u003ctd>487.5 (88 preemptions)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,213\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In the static run only 52% of sequence slots produced a token. A preemption costs a recomputation of P + G tokens (prompt plus generated) and, more importantly, latency for the victim; frequent preemptions are a sign the KV pool is too small for the admitted load.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Policy\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it buys\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it costs\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Static batching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>simplest; fine for offline jobs with equal lengths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>idle slots, long queueing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Iteration-level, reserve maximum KV at admission (Orca)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>never preempts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>admits far fewer requests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Iteration-level with paged KV and preemption (vLLM)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>high utilisation and large batches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recomputation and latency spikes under memory pressure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Priority queue instead of FIFO\u003c/td>\u003ctd>latency classes (interactive versus batch)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>starvation risk unless priorities age\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the continuous scheme for any online service with varied lengths. Tune two knobs: the token budget (small protects the latency of streams already running; large finishes prompts sooner) and the KV pool (too small and you preempt constantly).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Block size 2 tokens, 5 blocks, at most 3 running, budget 6 tokens per step. Requests (arrival step, prompt tokens, output tokens): A (0, 5, 3), B (0, 3, 4), C (1, 4, 3), D (2, 2, 2). Blocks are counted while the step runs.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 0: admit A with its whole prompt (5 tokens, 3 blocks) and B with a 1-token chunk; A emits its first token\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     \u003cmark>A:5  B:1\u003c/mark>          blocks 4/5\nrunning  A B                   waiting  -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 1: A decodes 1, B finishes its prompt (2); C arrived but its 3-token chunk needs 2 blocks and none is free\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     A:1  B:2              blocks \u003cmark>5/5\u003c/mark>\nrunning  A B                   waiting  \u003cmark>C\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 2: A needs a 4th block: preempt the newest running request, B, and requeue it at the front. A then finishes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     A:1                   \u003cmark>preempt B\u003c/mark>, finish A\nrunning  -                     waiting  \u003cmark>B\u003c/mark> C D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 3: no admission was allowed last step; now B is recomputed (prompt 3 + 1 generated = 4 tokens) and C starts\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     \u003cmark>B:4  C:2\u003c/mark>          blocks 3/5\nrunning  B C                   waiting  D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 4: B decodes, C finishes its prompt; D waits for blocks\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     B:1  C:2              blocks 5/5\nrunning  B C                   waiting  D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 5: C needs a 3rd block and is itself the newest: C preempts itself. B emits its 4th token and finishes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     B:1                   \u003cmark>preempt C\u003c/mark>, finish B\nrunning  -                     waiting  C D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Step 6: C is recomputed (4 + 1 = 5 tokens) and D gets the last budget token\u003c/p>\u003cpre>plan     \u003cmark>C:5  D:1\u003c/mark>          blocks 4/5\nrunning  C D                   waiting  -\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Steps 7 and 8: C finishes at step 7, D at step 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finish   A:2  B:5  C:\u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>  D:\u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With unlimited memory and budget every request would finish at arrival + output − 1: A 2, B 3, C 3, D 3. Memory pressure and the two preemptions cost B 2 steps and C 4.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\nclass Request:\n    def __init__(self, rid, arrival, prompt, output):\n        self.rid, self.arrival, self.prompt, self.output = rid, arrival, prompt, output\n        self.computed = 0        # tokens whose K and V are in the cache\n        self.generated = 0       # output tokens emitted so far\n        self.blocks = 0          # KV blocks held\n        self.finish = None\n\n    def need(self):\n        \"\"\"Tokens to compute before the next output token can be emitted.\"\"\"\n        return self.prompt + self.generated - self.computed\n\n\ndef serve(requests, max_seqs, budget, num_blocks, block_size, log=None):\n    \"\"\"Iteration-level scheduling: every step admits, runs and retires requests.\"\"\"\n    arrivals = deque(sorted(requests, key=lambda r: (r.arrival, r.rid)))\n    waiting, running = deque(), []\n    free, step = num_blocks, 0\n\n    def grow(r, n):              # blocks so r can hold computed + n tokens; False if short\n        nonlocal free\n        extra = math.ceil((r.computed + n) / block_size) - r.blocks\n        if extra &gt; free:\n            return False\n        free -= extra\n        r.blocks += extra\n        return True\n\n    def release(r):\n        nonlocal free\n        free += r.blocks\n        r.blocks = 0\n\n    while arrivals or waiting or running:\n        while arrivals and arrivals[0].arrival &lt;= step:\n            waiting.append(arrivals.popleft())\n        left, plan, events, preempted = budget, {}, [], False\n        i = 0\n        while i &lt; len(running) and left &gt; 0:           # 1. running requests first\n            r = running[i]\n            n = min(r.need(), left)\n            while not grow(r, n):                       # out of blocks: preempt the newest\n                victim = running.pop()                  # not yet planned this step\n                release(victim)\n                victim.computed = 0                     # recompute its KV later\n                waiting.appendleft(victim)\n                events.append(f\"preempt {victim.rid}\")\n                preempted = True\n                if victim is r:\n                    break\n            if r in running:\n                plan[r.rid] = n\n                left -= n\n                i += 1\n        while not preempted and waiting and left &gt; 0 and len(running) &lt; max_seqs:  # 2. admit, FCFS\n            r = waiting[0]\n            n = min(r.need(), left)\n            if not grow(r, n):\n                break\n            running.append(waiting.popleft())\n            plan[r.rid] = n\n            left -= n\n        held = num_blocks - free\n        for r in list(running):                         # 3. run one step, retire finished\n            if r.rid not in plan:\n                continue\n            r.computed += plan[r.rid]\n            if r.computed == r.prompt + r.generated:\n                r.generated += 1\n                if r.generated == r.output:\n                    r.finish = step\n                    running.remove(r)\n                    release(r)\n                    events.append(f\"finish {r.rid}\")\n        if log is not None:\n            log.append((step, plan, [x.rid for x in waiting], [x.rid for x in running], held, events))\n        step += 1\n    return requests\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>It assumes every single request fits in the pool on its own (real servers reject longer requests up front). Tested on 400 random workloads: the budget, the sequence cap and the pool are never exceeded, every request emits exactly its output length, and with unlimited resources each request finishes at arrival + output − 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Admitting before running.\u003c/b> If new prompts take the budget and blocks first, streams already running stall or get preempted; plan running requests first, as vLLM does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the reset.\u003c/b> A preempted request's KV is gone; if its computed count is not reset to zero, later steps read freed blocks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Preempting the request you are planning.\u003c/b> The victim can be the current request itself; handle that case or the loop never ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thrashing.\u003c/b> Admitting new work in the step that just preempted refills the memory you freed and preempts again next step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Head-of-line blocking.\u003c/b> Strict first come first served stops at the first request that does not fit, even if a smaller one behind it would. That is the price of fairness; skipping ahead raises utilisation but can starve large requests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Starvation under priorities.\u003c/b> Low-priority requests can wait forever unless priority ages with waiting time (SGLang, for example, offers a highest-response-ratio-next policy).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In step 1 of the trace a sequence slot and 3 tokens of budget were free. Why was C not admitted?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Admission needs blocks for the first chunk: 3 tokens at block size 2 is 2 blocks, and all 5 blocks were held by A and B. The scheduler stops at the first waiting request that does not fit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the scheduler preempt the newest running request rather than the oldest?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Serving in arrival order means the earliest requests must keep making progress, so the latest arrivals give way first; otherwise a stream of new requests could starve old ones. The newest request has also usually done the least work, so recomputing it is cheapest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Rerun the trace with 8 blocks instead of 5. What changes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No preemption happens. C is admitted at step 1 alongside A and B, D joins at step 3, and the finish steps become A 2, B 4, C 4, D 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A scheduler admits waiting requests before planning the running ones. What do users see under load?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Long new prompts take the token budget and the free blocks, so requests that are already streaming get fewer tokens per step or are preempted: output stalls mid-sentence and inter-token latency spikes. Planning running requests first protects them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Rerun the trace with 8 blocks. Expected: no preemptions; finish steps A 2, B 4, C 4, D 4.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rerun it with 5 blocks and a budget of 3 tokens per step. Expected: preemptions of B at step 3 and C at step 7; finish steps A 3, B 7, C 10, D 11.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rerun it with unlimited budget, blocks and slots. Expected: A 2, B 3, C 3, D 3, each equal to arrival + output − 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generate 200 requests with \u003ccode>rng = random.Random(2026)\u003c/code>, drawing for each request in this order: the arrival gap \u003ccode>rng.choice([0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3])\u003c/code> added to the previous arrival, the prompt \u003ccode>rng.randint(20, 200)\u003c/code> and the output \u003ccode>rng.randint(5, 120)\u003c/code>. Write a static-batching simulator (when idle, take up to 16 requests that have arrived; the whole prompt runs in one step; a batch lasts as long as its longest output) and compare mean latency (finish − arrival + 1) with \u003ccode>serve(max_seqs=16, budget=10**9, num_blocks=10**9, block_size=16)\u003c/code>. Expected: 670.4 steps static against 320.1 continuous, and makespans of 1,572 against 901 steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how an LLM server plans every step from a waiting queue, a token budget and a block pool, and when and whom it preempts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:llmserve/measure-prefix-cache-reuse-on-simulated-chat-tra":"\u003cp>You will build the workload, replay it through the \u003ccode>RadixCache\u003c/code> from the radix-tree card, and compare it with a vLLM-style block cache and with two scheduling orders. Everything is seeded, so your numbers should match the ones below exactly if you copy the generator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Put \u003ccode>Node\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>RadixCache\u003c/code> from the radix-tree card in \u003ccode>prefix_lab.py\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a workload generator: four apps with system prompts of 300, 500, 800 and 1,200 random token ids, 120 conversations of 1 to 6 turns (each user turn 20 to 80 tokens, each reply 50 to 200), interleaved at random while keeping each conversation's turns in order:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\ndef chat_workload(seed=0, apps=(300, 500, 800, 1200), conversations=120, vocab=50_000):\n    \"\"\"Requests (prompt, reply) from multi-turn chats that share per-app system prompts.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    systems = [[rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(n)] for n in apps]\n    weights = [8, 4, 2, 1]                                # some apps are busier than others\n    convs = []\n    for _ in range(conversations):\n        history = list(rng.choices(systems, weights)[0])\n        turns = []\n        for _ in range(rng.randint(1, 6)):\n            history += [rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(rng.randint(20, 80))]   # user turn\n            reply = [rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(rng.randint(50, 200))]\n            turns.append((list(history), reply))\n            history += reply\n        convs.append(turns)\n    stream, live = [], [c[:] for c in convs]              # interleave, keeping turn order\n    while live:\n        c = rng.choice(live)\n        stream.append(c.pop(0))\n        if not c:\n            live.remove(c)\n    return stream\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Replay the stream. For each request, count the matched prompt tokens, insert prompt plus reply, then evict down to the capacity while the current request's node is locked. The hit rate is reused prompt tokens divided by prompt tokens (SGLang's definition). Run capacities 2,000, 5,000, 10,000, 20,000, 50,000 and unlimited (\u003ccode>10**9\u003c/code>):\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def replay(stream, capacity):\n    cache = RadixCache()\n    hit = total = 0\n    for prompt, reply in stream:\n        h, _ = cache.match(prompt)\n        hit += h\n        total += len(prompt)\n        _, node = cache.insert(prompt + reply)\n        if cache.size &gt; capacity:\n            cache.lock(node, +1)                          # never evict the running request\n            cache.evict(cache.size - capacity)\n            cache.lock(node, -1)\n    return hit / total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Implement the vLLM-style alternative with B = 16: key each full block by (parent key, block tokens), look blocks up until the first miss, and keep an \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code> as an LRU of blocks. Touch a sequence's blocks from tail to head so that heads stay most recent (vLLM frees a request's blocks in reverse order for the same reason):\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def replay_blocks(stream, capacity, B=16):\n    \"\"\"vLLM-style: chained keys of full blocks, LRU over blocks.\"\"\"\n    lru = OrderedDict()\n    hit = total = 0\n    for prompt, reply in stream:\n        key, n = None, 0\n        for i in range(0, len(prompt) - B + 1, B):\n            key = (key, tuple(prompt[i:i + B]))\n            if key not in lru:\n                break\n            n += B\n        hit += n\n        total += len(prompt)\n        seq, key, keys = prompt + reply, None, []\n        for i in range(0, len(seq) - B + 1, B):\n            key = (key, tuple(seq[i:i + B]))\n            keys.append(key)\n        for k in reversed(keys):                          # tail first, so heads stay hotter\n            lru[k] = True\n            lru.move_to_end(k)\n        while len(lru) * B &gt; capacity:\n            lru.popitem(last=False)\n    return hit / total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Test scheduling order. Build a batch of 200 single-turn requests from 8 few-shot prompts of 600 tokens (25 questions of 40 tokens each, shuffled), all waiting at once. Serve them first in arrival order (FCFS), then always picking the waiting request with the longest cached prefix (longest prefix match, as SGLang's \u003ccode>lpm\u003c/code> policy does), at capacities 1,500, 3,000 and 6,000:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def few_shot_batch(seed=1, apps=8, per_app=25, shot_len=600, q_len=40, vocab=50_000):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    shots = [[rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(shot_len)] for _ in range(apps)]\n    reqs = [(shots[a] + [rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(q_len)], [rng.randrange(vocab) for _ in range(30)])\n            for a in range(apps) for _ in range(per_app)]\n    rng.shuffle(reqs)\n    return reqs\n\n\ndef replay_batch(reqs, capacity, policy):\n    cache = RadixCache()\n    waiting = list(reqs)\n    hit = total = 0\n    while waiting:\n        if policy == \"lpm\":\n            j = max(range(len(waiting)), key=lambda i: cache.match(waiting[i][0])[0])\n        else:\n            j = 0\n        prompt, reply = waiting.pop(j)\n        h, _ = cache.match(prompt)\n        hit += h\n        total += len(prompt)\n        _, node = cache.insert(prompt + reply)\n        if cache.size &gt; capacity:\n            cache.lock(node, +1)\n            cache.evict(cache.size - capacity)\n            cache.lock(node, -1)\n    return hit / total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Explain each result in two sentences in your notes: what limits reuse, and which data-structure choice causes it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The stream has 388 requests and 318,523 prompt tokens. The whole run takes a few seconds.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Capacity (tokens)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Radix tree\u003c/th>\u003cth>Blocks of 16\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>28.2%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32.8%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50.5%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>51.9%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>68.5%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>67.9%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>20,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80.9%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80.2%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>91.7%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>90.7%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>unlimited\u003c/td>\u003ctd>93.0%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>92.1%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The ceiling is 93%.\u003c/b> Even an unlimited cache must compute every system prompt once and every new user turn, which is the remaining 7%.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Token granularity wins when memory is plentiful.\u003c/b> The radix tree also reuses the partial last block of each prefix, so it leads by about one point at 50,000 and above.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Eviction granularity wins when memory is tight.\u003c/b> At 2,000 tokens the block cache is ahead: the radix tree evicts whole leaves, often a long conversation history at once, while block LRU trims tails 16 tokens at a time and keeps the hot heads.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scheduling order dominates under pressure.\u003c/b> With 1,500, 3,000 and 6,000 tokens of cache, FCFS reaches 22.0%, 38.4% and 84.4%, while longest prefix match reaches 90.0% each time. That is the best possible here: each app's 600-token prefix must be computed once and every 40-token question is new, so at most 8 × 24 × 600 = 115,200 of the 200 × 640 = 128,000 prompt tokens can be reused. Grouping requests that share a prefix is the depth-first order of SGLang's Theorem 3.1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
